{
  "ConnectionStrings": { "DefaultConnection": "Server=(localdb)\\MSSQLLocalDB;Database=EmployeeDb;Trusted_Connection=True;TrustServerCertificate=True;" },
  "Jwt": {
    "Key": "ThisIsADevelopmentOnlySecretKeyChangeItLater",
    "Issuer": "EmployeeManagement.API",
    "Audience": "EmployeeManagement.Client",
    "ExpiryMinutes": 60
  },
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
  },
  "AllowedHosts": "*"
}


/*
============================================================
APPSETTINGS.JSON - COMPLETE EXPLANATION
============================================================

This file contains configuration settings for the
EmployeeManagement.API application.

Instead of hard-coding values such as database connection
strings, JWT settings, and logging settings directly inside
C# classes, we keep them in appsettings.json.

This approach is called:

    Externalized Configuration

It makes the application easier to configure for different
environments such as:

    Development
    Testing
    Production


============================================================
1. CONNECTION STRINGS
============================================================

"ConnectionStrings": {
    "DefaultConnection": "..."
}

This section contains database connection information.

The key:

    "DefaultConnection"

is the name of our database connection.

In Program.cs we access this value using:

    builder.Configuration.GetConnectionString(
        "DefaultConnection")

This returns the connection string and is then used by
Entity Framework Core to connect to SQL Server.


------------------------------------------------------------
DATABASE CONNECTION STRING
------------------------------------------------------------

"Server=(localdb)\\MSSQLLocalDB;
 Database=EmployeeDb;
 Trusted_Connection=True;
 TrustServerCertificate=True;"

Each part has a specific purpose.


Server=(localdb)\\MSSQLLocalDB

This tells the application to connect to the local SQL Server
LocalDB instance named:

    MSSQLLocalDB

LocalDB is commonly used for local development.

It allows us to work with SQL Server without installing and
running a full SQL Server instance.


Database=EmployeeDb

This specifies the database name.

Our application uses:

    EmployeeDb

Inside this database we have tables such as:

    Employees
    Users
    __EFMigrationsHistory


Trusted_Connection=True

This means Windows authentication is used to connect to
SQL Server.

The current Windows user's credentials are used instead of
providing a SQL username and password in the connection string.


TrustServerCertificate=True

This tells the application to trust the SQL Server certificate.

This is commonly used in local development environments.

For production environments, certificate configuration should
be handled properly according to the organization's security
requirements.


============================================================
2. JWT CONFIGURATION
============================================================

"Jwt": {
    "Key": "...",
    "Issuer": "...",
    "Audience": "...",
    "ExpiryMinutes": 60
}

This section contains configuration required for JWT
authentication.

Our JwtService reads these values when generating JWT tokens.


------------------------------------------------------------
Jwt:Key
------------------------------------------------------------

"Key": "ThisIsADevelopmentOnlySecretKeyChangeItLater"

This is the secret key used to digitally sign the JWT.

In JwtService, we read it using:

    _configuration["Jwt:Key"]

The key is then converted into bytes and used to create:

    SymmetricSecurityKey

The same secret key is required when the API validates
incoming JWT tokens.

IMPORTANT:

This value is only suitable as a development placeholder.

A real production secret should NOT be hard-coded in
appsettings.json or committed to source control.

Production applications should use a secure secret-management
solution such as environment variables or a cloud secret store.


------------------------------------------------------------
Jwt:Issuer
------------------------------------------------------------

"Issuer": "EmployeeManagement.API"

Issuer identifies who created or issued the JWT token.

In our application:

    EmployeeManagement.API

is the issuer.

JwtService passes this value when creating the token:

    issuer: issuer

During JWT validation, the API can verify that the token
was issued by the expected issuer.


------------------------------------------------------------
Jwt:Audience
------------------------------------------------------------

"Audience": "EmployeeManagement.Client"

Audience identifies who the JWT token is intended for.

In our application:

    EmployeeManagement.Client

is the configured audience.

JwtService adds this value to the JWT.

During authentication, the API can validate that the token
was created for the expected audience.


------------------------------------------------------------
Jwt:ExpiryMinutes
------------------------------------------------------------

"ExpiryMinutes": 60

This specifies how long the JWT token should remain valid.

In JwtService:

    DateTime.UtcNow.AddMinutes(expiryMinutes)

If the value is:

    60

the token expires 60 minutes after it is generated.

After expiration, the client must authenticate again or use
the application's configured token-refresh mechanism if one
exists.


============================================================
3. LOGGING CONFIGURATION
============================================================

"Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
        "Default": "Information",
        "Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"
    }
}

This section controls application logging levels.


------------------------------------------------------------
Default: Information
------------------------------------------------------------

"Default": "Information"

This sets the default minimum logging level to Information.

For example, our EmployeeService contains:

    _logger.LogInformation(...)

These messages can be written to the configured logging
provider.


Common logging levels include:

    Trace
    Debug
    Information
    Warning
    Error
    Critical


They represent increasing severity.


------------------------------------------------------------
Microsoft.AspNetCore: Warning
------------------------------------------------------------

"Microsoft.AspNetCore": "Warning"

This sets the minimum log level for Microsoft.AspNetCore
components to Warning.

This helps reduce unnecessary framework-level Information
logs while still allowing warnings and errors to be logged.

For example:

    Application code
        → Information logs enabled

    ASP.NET Core framework
        → Warning and above


============================================================
4. ALLOWED HOSTS
============================================================

"AllowedHosts": "*"

This controls which host names are allowed to access the
application.

The value:

    "*"

means all host names are allowed.

This is commonly seen in development projects.

In production, host filtering should be configured according
to the application's deployment and security requirements.


============================================================
HOW THIS FILE CONNECTS TO YOUR APPLICATION
============================================================

The configuration values in this file are consumed by
different parts of the application.

Database:

    appsettings.json
          ↓
    ConnectionStrings
          ↓
    Program.cs
          ↓
    AppDbContext
          ↓
    Entity Framework Core
          ↓
    SQL Server


JWT:

    appsettings.json
          ↓
    Jwt configuration
          ↓
    JwtService
          ↓
    GenerateToken()
          ↓
    JWT Token
          ↓
    Client
          ↓
    Authorization: Bearer <token>


Logging:

    appsettings.json
          ↓
    Logging configuration
          ↓
    ILogger
          ↓
    EmployeeService / AuthService / Middleware
          ↓
    Application logs


============================================================
INTERVIEW-READY EXPLANATION
============================================================

"appsettings.json is used to store application configuration
outside the source code. In my project, I use it for the SQL
Server connection string, JWT configuration, logging levels,
and allowed hosts.

The ConnectionStrings section contains the LocalDB connection
information for my EmployeeDb database.

The Jwt section contains the signing key, issuer, audience,
and token expiration time. My JwtService reads these values
using IConfiguration when generating JWT tokens.

The Logging section controls the minimum logging level, and
AllowedHosts controls which host names are accepted.

This approach is called externalized configuration and makes
it easier to maintain different configuration values for
different environments without changing the application code."

============================================================
*/